{
  "passage": "India is a secular country. In the Indian Constitution, secularism means that the State does not favour any one religion, and that every person is free to follow, practise and spread the religion of their choice. The State does not have an official religion. However, Indian secularism is different from the idea of a complete wall between the State and religion that some other countries follow. In India, the State keeps what is called a principled distance from religion. This means that it usually stays away from religious matters, but it can step in when a religious practice goes against the rights guaranteed by the Constitution. For example, the Constitution abolished untouchability, even though some people defended it as part of their religious tradition. The State can also make exceptions out of respect for religious practices. The law requires people riding two-wheelers to wear helmets, but Sikhs who wear turbans are allowed not to wear a helmet. The aim of all this is to make sure that no religious group dominates another, that members of the same religion do not dominate each other, and that the State does not force any religion on people. In government schools, therefore, the celebration of a religious festival as an official school event is not allowed, but students are free to celebrate festivals at home.",
